        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Where San Jose homeowners put glass railings</strong></p><p>San Jose spreads across the Santa Clara Valley, with the Diablo Range to the east and the Santa Cruz Mountains to the west. That geography shapes the projects we ship. In Willow Glen and the Rose Garden, older homes get second-floor additions with a balcony that needs a guard. Almaden Valley and Evergreen sit against the hills, so decks there often step out over a slope and face a view. A frameless glass deck railing keeps that view open where a picket guard would cut it into strips.</p><p>Closer to downtown, newer townhomes with roof decks use glass for the parapet guard. Modern remodels in Cambrian Park and Los Gatos often replace a wood stair guard with glass. We also ship panels for the balconies and exterior stairs of ADUs, a common addition on San Jose lots.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>The 42\" rule and what it means for your order</strong></p><p>The California Residential Code sets a 42\" minimum guard height, even for a single-family house. It applies where a deck or balcony floor is more than 30\" above grade. The City of San Jose applies the 2022 edition of Title 24, and permits for new decks and balconies go through its building division. Check with them before you order, and read the <a href=\"/pages/california-building-standards-code-for-glass-railings\">California code page</a> for the full summary.</p><p>For most San Jose houses the choice is between the 39.6\" panel, which gives a 42\" railing, and the 46\" panel, which gives 48\". The taller panel adds a margin above the minimum and reads as a cleaner line on a view deck. Each panel sits in two spigots, so there are no posts. The spigots bolt to the joists or the slab, and the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-engineering-testing\">engineering documents</a> cover both wood and concrete.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery and installation in San Jose</strong></p><p>Orders are processed in 2–3 business days. The crate then travels 5–10 business days by freight carrier to San Jose, with liftgate delivery at the curb. The price you see at checkout is final: no duties, tariffs or hidden fees, even though the order crosses from Canada. Orders over 3,000 USD ship free. Check the crate on arrival and report any damage within 3 days, before installation, and we replace or refund the item at no cost.</p><p>The system is designed for DIY installation. Most standard installations take about 4 hours with the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-installation-guide\">installation guide</a> and videos. Spigots fix to wood or concrete with screws sold separately. San Jose summers are warm and dry, so the outdoor work season is long; pick a day when the deck surface is clear and someone can help you lift the panels.</p>"
          }
        }
